## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|---------|----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` without cancellation | Can't be woken cooperatively |

Additionally, a **race condition** exists: multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals, dequeuing only one item.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();

        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _workerTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Graceful drain timed out — force cancel
                _cts?.Cancel();
                await _workerTask.WaitAsync(CancellationToken.None);
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

## If you need a synchronous `Stop()` (no async callers)

```csharp
public void Stop()
{
    _channel.Writer.Complete();
    _cts?.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    _workerTask?.Wait(); // blocks until loop exits
    _cts?.Dispose();
}
```

---

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Forgetting `Channel.Writer.Complete()`** | Without it, `ReadAllAsync` never finishes — your worker hangs forever |
| **Not passing `CancellationToken` to `Handle`** | If `Handle` does I/O or long work, pass the token so it can exit promptly |
| **Race in original code** | The original loses events when multiple `Enqueue` calls happen before `Reset`. `Channel<T>` eliminates this entirely |
| **Disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always dispose — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks |
| **`Thread.Abort` fallback** | There is no equivalent in .NET 8. Design `Handle()` to check cancellation cooperatively |

---

## Why `Channel<T>` over manual lock + queue

`System.Threading.Channels` replaces your `Queue` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` with a single thread-safe, backpressure-aware primitive. It's the idiomatic .NET 8 producer/consumer pattern — no manual synchronization needed.
